    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nEver since I started using nvim 0.12, I get errors in nvim's diagnostics code. I added code to help track this down:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua b/runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua\nindex f5e6eb3c92..89e6911a46 100644\n--- a/runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua\n+++ b/runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua\n@@ -1852,6 +1852,10 @@ M.handlers.underline = {\n           api.nvim_buf_get_lines(diagnostic.bufnr, diagnostic.lnum, diagnostic.lnum + 1, true)\n \n         for _, higroup in ipairs(higroups) do\n+          if not lines[1] then\n+            local k,v = next(lines)\n+            error(string.format(\"%s\\nbufnr=%s, diagnostic.lnum=%s #lines=%s lines[%s][%s]\", \"show_once_loaded had no lines\", diagnostic.bufnr, diagnostic.lnum, #lines, k, v))\n+          end\n           vim.hl.range(\n             bufnr,\n             underline_ns,\n```\n\nToday I got a hit when using [unite](https://github.com/Shougo/unite.vim) to open a file:\n```\n[unite.vim] function unite#action#do[70]..92[3]..BufReadPost Autocommands for \"<buffer=232>\"\n[unite.vim] Vim(append):Lua callback: ...neovim\\current\\share\\nvim\\runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua:1857: show_once_loaded had no lines\n[unite.vim] bufnr=232, diagnostic.lnum=-1 #lines=0 lines[nil][nil]\n[unite.vim] stack traceback:\n[unite.vim]   [C]: in function 'error'\n[unite.vim]   ...neovim\\current\\share\\nvim\\runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua:1857: in function 'fn'\n[unite.vim]   ...neovim\\current\\share\\nvim\\runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua:1747: in function 'fn'\n[unite.vim]   ...neovim\\current\\share\\nvim\\runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua:1356: in function <...neovim\\current\\share\\nvim\\runtime/lua/vim/diagnostic.lua:1355>\n[unite.vim] Error occurred while executing \"open\" action!\n```\nThat buffer is the one I was jumping to and one I'd previously had open in this session, but nothing was modifying it:\n```\n:ls\n...\n232 %a x \"c:\\code\\project\\scripts\\strings\\strings_npc1.lua\" line 76\n...\n```\n`:help :ls` says the second column means it had read errors:\n```\n\t\t %\tthe buffer in the current window\n\t\t  a\tan active buffer: it is loaded and visible\n\t\t    x   a buffer with read errors\n```\nBut if I `:edit` that file, then the `x` goes away, so maybe that's not helpful.\n\n----\nI usually hit errors like this when using the quickfix or unite to jump to a file -- but these are the primary ways I jump to files, so that's not surprising. I hit this or a similar error 5-10 times during an 8 hour day, so I have no idea how to trigger it but it's very annoying.\n\nSo it seems like maybe the lsp is sending invalid diagnostics (negative line number)? Is there anything else I can do to help track this down?\n\n(I have also tried nvim-nightly but found it far too unstable to use regularly.)\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nI work in a large lua project and use [luals](https://luals.github.io/) installed via [Mason](https://github.com/mason-org/mason.nvim/).\n\n    ◍ lua-language-server lua_ls 3.18.2\n\n(3.19.0 is available. I'll update and see if this still occurs.)\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nnvim never errors even if diagnostics are invalid.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno, it doesn't have diagnostics\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nWin11\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nneovide\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nn/a\n\n### Installation\n\nscoop",
